> I read this as research and architectural design - analyzing the `TerminalService` requirements in `.omo/plans/rorca-remote-terminal.md` and defining the key Rust structs and signatures.

---

### 1. Architectural Analysis of `TerminalService`

In the current codebase, Tauri commands (`src-tauri/src/ipc/terminal.rs`) directly orchestrate `WorkspaceRegistry`, `PtyManager`, and Tauri event emission (`terminal_output`, `terminal_lifecycle`). This couples PTY management to Tauri IPC and a single consumer per PTY stream (`mpsc::Receiver`).

`TerminalService` introduces an transport-agnostic coordination layer that fulfills several design requirements from `.omo/plans/rorca-remote-terminal.md`:

```text
[Tauri IPC]       [RemoteGateway (WebSocket)]
     \                  /
      ▼                ▼
       TerminalService
      /       |        \
     ▼        ▼         ▼
Workspace  PtyManager  TerminalOutputHub
Registry               (Broadcast + RingBuffer)
```

#### Key Invariants & Responsibilities
1. **Safety Boundary Enforcement**: Spawning must go through `WorkspaceRegistry::resolve_terminal_target` and acquire a `WriterLeaseGuard` on dirty worktrees before PTY creation. Remote clients cannot spawn arbitrary shell paths or bypass worktree locks.
2. **Output Fan-Out & Multi-Consumer Attach**: PTY stdout/stderr is pumped into a `TerminalOutputHub` using `tokio::sync::broadcast` so Desktop Tauri UI and multiple remote clients (phone/tablet) can observe the same session concurrently.
3. **Bounded Replay on Attach**: Late-attaching clients receive an atomic snapshot of recent terminal output (512 KiB – 1 MiB ring buffer in memory) followed by live broadcast frames without lost or duplicated bytes.
4. **Non-blocking Backpressure**: A slow or lagging remote subscriber must never block the PTY reader pump or degrade local desktop terminal responsiveness.
5. **Session Lifecycle & Cleanup**: Closing a session reaps child processes, drops the worktree `WriterLeaseGuard`, cleans up broadcast channels, and broadcasts lifecycle state changes (`Started`, `Exited`, `Failed`).

